/*!
  \mainpage notitle

  \section ponca_about_sec About Ponca

  %Ponca (POiNt Cloud Analysis) is a header-only C++20 library for point cloud analysis and surface reconstruction,
  compatible with CUDA and SYCL, and available in Python through the PyPonca binding. It is maintained by researchers
  in Computer Graphics, and released under the Mozilla Public License 2.0. The source code is available at
  https://github.com/poncateam/ponca.

  %Ponca provides a collection of techniques for point cloud analysis that incorporate the latest innovations from
  CNRS and INRIA research teams working in the field. It strives for efficiency and ease of use by focusing on low-level
  core operators and key algorithms. The central goal of the library is to drastically reduce the time and effort
  required to turn a research paper into a ready-to-use solution, for both commercial and academic purposes.

  \section ponca_features_sec Features

  - **Local fitting** of planes, lines, algebraic spheres and quadratic height fields on weighted neighborhoods, in
    arbitrary dimension, from positions with or without (oriented or unoriented) normals.
  - **Differential quantities**: normals, Growing Least Squares descriptors, curvature tensors (APSS, ASO, Monge patches,
    Corrected Normal Current, ...), and the derivatives of the fits with respect to the scale and to the position.
  - **Moving Least Squares** projection onto the smooth surface defined by a point cloud.
  - **Spatial data structures**: kd-trees, k-nearest neighbor graphs and neighbor graphs, with nearest, k-nearest and
    range queries.
  - **Generic and extensible design**: no data structure is imposed on the client code, computations are assembled at
    compile time, and the same code runs on the CPU, in CUDA and in SYCL kernels.

  \section ponca_doc_map_sec Documentation

  - \subpage ponca_getting_started_page &mdash; requirements, installation and a first program.
  - \subpage user_manual_page &mdash; how to use the modules of the library, and the Python binding.
  - \subpage example_page &mdash; complete programs, in C++, CUDA, SYCL and Python.
  - \subpage ponca_ecosystem_page &mdash; tools and libraries built on top of %Ponca.
  - \subpage ponca_changelog &mdash; what changed between the releases.
  - \ref citelist "Bibliography" &mdash; the scientific publications implemented in the library.
  - <a href="annotated.html">C++ API reference</a> &mdash; the documentation of all the classes.

  \section ponca_instructions_sec Frequently asked questions

  - <b>How do I install %Ponca?</b><br/>
  %Ponca is header-only: see \ref ponca_getting_started_page.

  - <b>What can I develop with %Ponca? Are there code examples?</b><br/>
  The \ref user_manual_page describes all the features of the library, and the \ref example_page page lists complete
  programs.

  - <b>Do I need to convert my data to use %Ponca?</b><br/>
  No: %Ponca accesses your points through a lightweight type that you define, which can directly wrap your buffers.
  See \ref ponca_points.

  - <b>Where do these approaches come from?</b><br/>
  %Ponca implements published scientific papers, listed in the \ref citelist "Bibliography". They are useful to
  understand the implemented operators and algorithms. If %Ponca contributes to a project that leads to a scientific
  publication or to a commercial or free software, all we ask is to tell us about it, or at least to cite the project
  using the BibTeX entry given in \ref ponca_credits_subsection_citation.

  - <b>What can I do if I get stuck?</b><br/>
  If you get stuck or find a bug, open an issue on <a href="https://github.com/poncateam/ponca/issues"
  target="_blank">GitHub</a>. The library is under active development, and we are pleased to get your feedback.

  - <b>What does "Ponca" mean?</b><br/>
  "Ponca" stands for POiNt Cloud Analysis.

  - <b>How is %Ponca licensed?</b><br/>
  The source code of the library is licensed under the Mozilla Public License (MPL), v. 2.0. Its main advantage is to
  allow the inclusion of template files as long as the source code itself is not modified. A copy of the MPL can be
  found at http://mozilla.org/MPL/2.0/.

